    "data": {
      "A": "Own a serial algorithmic candidate: replace per-bucket quadratic insertion sort with a stable bytewise LSD radix sort of the stored tokens. Sort by unsigned-byte lexicographic order, not parsed numeric values, so leading zeros and emitted text are preserved. Make short records NUL-padded or use a sentinel-aware accessor so behavior remains equivalent to strcmp for every valid declared width; remove the source's implicit balanced-bucket-capacity assumption. Report correctness and same-harness timing.",
      "B": "Own a 4-core parallel compute candidate: implement a race-free parallel stable radix pass with per-thread 256-bin histograms, prefix offsets, and scatter into alternate index buffers. Honor available thread settings, avoid oversubscription, and remain correct for skewed first-byte distributions rather than merely parallelizing the original buckets. Compare one-thread and target-core behavior and retain only a measured win; preserve exact sorted textual output.",
      "C": "Own the composable I/O/data-movement candidate. Replace fscanf/printf-heavy paths with a buffered raw-token reader and buffered/chunked writer while retaining each token's original bytes, NUL termination, whitespace parsing, and one output line per item. Keep a simple contiguous record/index interface suitable for A or B, initialize padding needed by radix ordering, and measure whether I/O remains material after linear sorting. Do not use numeric conversion or benchmark-specific shortcuts.",
      "G": "Integrate empirically, not additively. Start from the fastest valid standalone result from A/B/C, merge only clearly orthogonal verified changes, and re-run the prescribed correctness cases plus identical performance measurements after every merge. Compare the final candidate against every contributing standalone candidate and baseline; keep the fastest valid one, since parallelism or I/O integration may regress it. Preserve all valid-input semantics, output bytes/order, and existing harness/compiler/target constraints.",
      "analysis": "The only measurement is a correct 0.419728 s mean baseline on 4-core ARM64. Its dominant algorithmic risk is O(bucket_size^2) insertion sorting with repeated strcmp calls; the fixed-width textual input makes a general radix ordering the primary hypothesis. The source also assumes roughly balanced bucket populations, so no candidate may rely on that distribution. Once sorting is linear, token parsing and formatted output may become limiting, while four-way parallel radix may help but must be measured rather than presumed."
